{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>הִנֵּה נָא הוֹאַלְתִּי לְדַבֵּר אֶל ה׳ וְאָנֹכִי עָפָר וָאֵפֶר.</b> 'הוֹאַלְתִּי' is a term of beginning, as Rashi explained on the verse ״הוֹאִיל מֹשֶׁה בֵּאֵר אֶת הַתּוֹרָה״ (Deuteronomy 1:5), and he brought a proof from ״הִנֵּה נָא הוֹאַלְתִּי.״ Now, we already said above that the Holy One, blessed be He, seated Abraham in judgment over the men of Sodom, and in capital cases they begin from the side. Therefore he said, 'הִנֵּה נָא הוֹאַלְתִּי' — and why am I beginning to express my opinion? Because I am dust and ashes, and in capital cases they begin from the side. And this is why Rashi specifically explained 'הוֹאַלְתִּי' as it is said the second time (v. 31), meaning 'I wished,' as in ״וַיּוֹאֶל מֹשֶׁה״ (Exodus 2:21), whereas in the parashah of Devarim Rashi explained 'הוֹאִיל מֹשֶׁה' as 'he began,' like 'הוֹאַלְתִּי לְדַבֵּר' — [these two explanations] are difficult one against the other. Rather, it is certain that here Rashi was explaining the 'הוֹאַלְתִּי' said the second time, and there he was explaining the 'הוֹאַלְתִּי' said the first time, for the term bears both meanings. And this also accounts for why 'וְאָנֹכִי עָפָר וָאֵפֶר' was not said with the second 'הוֹאַלְתִּי' — and it is easy to understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The final phrase 'וקל להבין' implies the reader can readily see why the reasoning about beginning from the side only applies to the first instance, since by the second instance Abraham has already begun speaking."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:28:47.381822230Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}